>> title: Sesame Ginger Chicken
>> servings: 4
>> source: https://www.budgetbytes.com/easy-sesame-chicken

== Sauce ==

In a #small bowl{} stir together @soy sauce{0.25%cup}, @water{2%tbsp}, @toasted sesame oil{1%tbsp}, @brown sugar{3%tbsp}, @rice vinegar{1%tbsp}, @fresh ginger{1%tsp}, @garlic{2%cloves}(minced), @cornstarch{0.5%tbsp}, and @sesame seeds{1%tbsp}. 

Set the sauce aside.

== Chicken ==

In a #large bowl{}, whisk together @egg{1}(large), @cornstarch{2%tbsp}, and @salt{pinch} and @black pepper{pinch}. 

Trim any excess fat from @boneless skinless chicken thighs{1%lb}, then cut them into small 1 inch pieces. Toss the chicken in the egg and cornstarch mixture.

Add @cooking oil{2%tbsp} to a large #skillet{} and heat it over medium flame. Wait until the skillet is very hot, then swirl the skillet to make sure the oil coats the entire surface. 

Add the batter coated chicken and spread it out into a single layer over the surface of the skillet.

Allow the chicken pieces to cook, undisturbed, until golden brown on the bottom. Then, carefully flip the chicken, breaking up the pieces into smaller clumps as you flip. Continue to cook the chicken until golden brown on the other side. Stir the chicken as little as possible to avoid breaking the egg coating from the surface of the chicken.

Once the chicken is cooked through and golden brown on all sides, pour the sauce over top. Toss the chicken to coat in the sauce. As the sauce comes up to a simmer, it will begin to thicken. Continue to gently stir the chicken in the sauce until it has thickened, then turn off the heat.